{%- comment -%}
  OnRepeat — knit-family rail.

  A horizontal row of chips under the collection hero that splits a large,
  visually uniform range into the families it is actually designed around.
  Polo is 48 products that are really 8 knits — CANE, CORD, FERN, GRID, HIVE,
  LEAF, TILE, VANE — in 6 colourways each. Without the rail, browsing that is
  scrolling; with it, it is eight decisions of six.

  Renders nothing at all unless a collection genuinely has families, so this
  can sit in the shared collection section without giving every other
  collection a stray widget. See "WHEN THIS APPEARS" below.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  WHERE THE FAMILIES COME FROM

  `collection.all_tags`, filtered by shape. A family tag is:

    1. a single word            — excludes "KNIT POLO"
    2. all uppercase            — excludes the colourways (Slate, Olive, Navy,
                                  Camel, Black, Cream), which are Title Case
    3. not the category itself  — excludes "POLOS" on the Polo collection, by
                                  testing containment against the collection
                                  title both ways ("POLOS" contains "POLO";
                                  "SHIRTS" contains "SHIRT")

  `all_tags` is used rather than a pass over product titles because it is the
  one source that stays complete once a shopper has *selected* a family: on
  /collections/polo/cane, `collection.products` is filtered down to the six
  CANE pieces, and deriving the list from those would collapse the rail to a
  single chip the moment it was used.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  WHEN THIS APPEARS

  Two guards, both of which have to pass:

    • at least 2 families that each hold at least 2 products
    • the collection fits inside the 50-product window Liquid gives a `for`
      loop, so the counts are whole rather than truncated

  That is what keeps the rail on Polo (8 × 6) and off everything else. T-Shirts
  is 20 products with 20 distinct one-word names (Trinetra, Petra, AXIS…), so
  every candidate family holds exactly 1 and the rail suppresses itself.
  Hoodies (5) and Shirts (4) behave the same way. All Products is 92, past the
  window, so it suppresses too. No per-collection setting, and no list of
  handles to keep in sync with the catalogue.

  The first guard is skipped once a family is open, because it cannot be
  answered from there — see COUNTS. The row has to survive being used.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  WHERE THE CHIPS LINK

  {{ collection.url }}/{{ tag | handleize }} — Shopify's own tag view of a
  collection, e.g. /collections/polo/cane. Verified against the live store: it
  returns exactly the six CANE colourways and leaves the storefront filters
  intact, so a shopper can still narrow by size or availability after choosing
  a knit. It needs no Search & Discovery setup — the store defines no
  product-tag filter, so a ?filter.p.tag= link would silently return the whole
  collection — and each family gets a real, indexable URL.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  COUNTS

  Counted from `collection.products` on the unfiltered view only. Once a
  family is open, Shopify has narrowed that list to the six pieces in it, so
  the other families are simply not countable from there — and the whole row
  drops its numbers rather than showing 6 against the open knit and nothing
  against the rest. The "All" number is `all_products_count`, which ignores
  tag filtering, so it stays at 48 throughout.

  "All" is a literal rather than a translation key: the theme defines no
  `collections.general.all`, and a missing key renders the string
  "Translation missing: …" in every locale (it is truthy, so `| default:`
  does not catch it). The family names beside it are untranslated proper
  nouns, so the row stays consistent.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  THE FALLBACK: CATEGORIES

  Everything above derives families from within one collection, which is why
  it goes quiet on the collections that need a rail most. All Products is 92
  pieces past the counting window; T-Shirts is 24 products with 24 distinct
  names. Those pages had no rail at all — nothing at the top of the page said
  what else there was to look at.

  So when no families can be found, the rail falls back to the shop's
  categories, taken from a menu the merchant already maintains. A menu is the
  right source precisely because it is not derived: it is the merchandising
  order someone chose, it is edited in the admin rather than here, and it is
  already what the mobile nav shows.

  Only top-level items that resolve to a real collection are used. That keeps
  a link to a page, a blog, or a collection handle that no longer exists from
  becoming a dead chip — the menu is edited by hand and one of those is a
  matter of time. This store's Shop menu is a live example: every item in it
  is a hand-typed URL, so `link.type` is `http_link` and `link.object` is nil
  on all five, and one of the five points at /collections/all-products, which
  is not a collection that exists.

  So the handle is taken from `link.object` when the merchant made a real
  collection link, and read out of the URL when they typed one — then looked
  up in `collections`, which is what actually proves the target is there.

  The chip's label is the MENU's title, not the collection's. "The Core
  Edition" and "Best Sellers" are what someone chose to call these in the nav;
  "OnRepeat's Core Edition" and "Summer | Best Sellers by OnRepeat" are their
  internal names. Counts come from the collection, so they are real totals
  rather than anything derived from this page.

  Params:
    collection    (required)
    fallback_menu (optional) — a link_list. Defaults to the "Categories" menu
                  (handle `categories`), which exists for this row: Polos,
                  T-Shirts, Sweatshirts, Joggers, Hoodies, Shirts, in
                  descending size. Falls through to the mobile Shop menu and
                  then the main menu only if that one is renamed or removed.

  Usage:
    {%- render 'onrepeat-family-rail', collection: collection -%}
    {%- render 'onrepeat-family-rail', collection: collection, fallback_menu: section.settings.category_menu -%}
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign fr_window = 50
  assign fr_total = collection.all_products_count
  assign fr_stem = collection.title | upcase | strip

  comment
    Pass 1 — candidate families, by tag shape. Complete on every view.
  endcomment
  assign fr_names = ''
  if fr_total > 0 and fr_total <= fr_window
    for fr_tag in collection.all_tags
      assign fr_word_test = fr_tag | strip | split: ' '
      if fr_word_test.size > 1
        continue
      endif

      assign fr_up = fr_tag | upcase
      unless fr_tag == fr_up
        continue
      endunless

      if fr_stem contains fr_up or fr_up contains fr_stem
        continue
      endif

      assign fr_names = fr_names | append: fr_tag | append: '|'
    endfor
  endif
  assign fr_list = fr_names | split: '|'

  comment
    Is a family currently selected? `current_tags` is nil rather than an empty
    array away from a tag view, and nil.size is nil — not 0 — so it has to be
    tested through `first`, which is blank in both cases.
  endcomment
  assign fr_active_tag = current_tags | first
  assign fr_filtered = false
  if fr_active_tag != blank
    assign fr_filtered = true
  endif

  comment
    Pass 2 — how many candidates actually group more than one product. A family
    of one is a product name, not a family, and a collection made entirely of
    those gets no rail at all.

    This can only be asked of an unfiltered view. On /collections/polo/cane,
    `collection.products` is the six CANE pieces, so every other family counts
    zero and the guard would tear the rail down at precisely the moment it is
    most useful — the shopper who picked a knit could not switch to another
    one without going back. So on a tag view the guard is skipped and the
    shape rules stand on their own: `all_tags` is unfiltered, so the list is
    still whole. Counts are suppressed there for the same reason, rather than
    printing 6 beside the open family and nothing beside the rest.
  endcomment
  assign fr_real = 0
  if fr_filtered
    assign fr_real = fr_list.size
  else
    for fr_name in fr_list
      assign fr_count = 0
      for fr_product in collection.products
        if fr_product.tags contains fr_name
          assign fr_count = fr_count | plus: 1
        endif
      endfor
      if fr_count > 1
        assign fr_real = fr_real | plus: 1
      endif
    endfor
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  ── The category fallback ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Resolved before the markup so the two paths can share one <nav>: whichever
  set of chips this ends up drawing, it is the same row, the same styles and
  the same docking behaviour. Only the source of the chips differs.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign fr_menu = fallback_menu
  comment
    "Categories" is the menu made for this row: six garment types, in
    descending size, every item a real collection link. It is the default
    rather than something that has to be chosen in the theme editor, because
    the row is useless empty and a setting nobody sets is a row nobody sees.
    The two behind it are only there so the rail still finds something if that
    menu is renamed or deleted.
  endcomment
  if fr_menu == blank or fr_menu.links.size == 0
    assign fr_menu = linklists['categories']
  endif
  if fr_menu == blank or fr_menu.links.size == 0
    assign fr_menu = linklists['main-menu-mobile']
  endif
  if fr_menu == blank or fr_menu.links.size == 0
    assign fr_menu = linklists['main-menu']
  endif

  assign fr_cats = 0
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The chips are built before it is known whether they will be printed, because
  the decision depends on how many survive: one category chip is not a
  category rail, and a menu of pages and blog links would otherwise draw an
  empty row. Counting in a first pass and rendering in a second would mean the
  resolution below — object, else URL, then prove it against `collections` —
  written out twice, where the two copies have to agree forever. `assign`
  inside `capture` writes to the outer scope, so one pass does both.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture fr_cat_chips -%}
  {%- if fr_real < 2 -%}
    {%- for fr_link in fr_menu.links -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign fr_handle = ''
        if fr_link.type == 'collection_link' and fr_link.object != blank
          assign fr_handle = fr_link.object.handle
        else
          comment
            A typed URL. Take the segment after /collections/ and stop at the
            next slash, so a tag view (/collections/polo/cane) resolves to its
            collection rather than to nothing. Query and fragment are dropped
            first — /collections/polo?sort_by=… is still Polo.
          endcomment
          assign fr_path = fr_link.url | split: '?' | first | split: '#' | first
          if fr_path contains '/collections/'
            assign fr_tail = fr_path | split: '/collections/' | last
            assign fr_handle = fr_tail | split: '/' | first
          endif
        endif

        comment
          The lookup is the proof of two separate things.

          That the collection exists at all: a handle that is no longer one —
          /collections/all-products on this store — comes back blank here.

          And that it has anything in it. `all_products_count` is the
          STOREFRONT count, so a collection whose products are all still
          drafts reads 0 even though the admin says otherwise. Joggers is
          exactly that today: six pairs, all unpublished. A chip that lands on
          an empty collection is worse than no chip, so the count has to be
          positive too — and the moment those products go live the chip
          appears on its own, with no menu to edit.
        endcomment
        assign fr_cat = blank
        if fr_handle != blank
          assign fr_cat = collections[fr_handle]
        endif
        assign fr_ok = false
        if fr_cat != blank and fr_cat.id != blank and fr_cat.all_products_count > 0
          assign fr_ok = true
        endif

        assign fr_here = false
        if fr_ok and fr_cat.handle == collection.handle
          assign fr_here = true
        endif
      -%}
      {%- if fr_ok -%}
        {%- assign fr_cats = fr_cats | plus: 1 -%}
        <a
          class="onrepeat-rail__chip{% if fr_here %} is-on{% endif %}"
          href="{{ fr_cat.url }}"
          {%- if fr_here %} aria-current="true"{% endif -%}
        >
          {{- fr_link.title -}}
          <span class="onrepeat-rail__n">{{ fr_cat.all_products_count }}</span>
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- if fr_real > 1 -%}
  {{ 'onrepeat-collection.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <nav class="onrepeat-rail" aria-label="{{ collection.title | escape }} — browse by knit">
    <div class="onrepeat-rail__track">
      <a
        class="onrepeat-rail__chip{% unless fr_filtered %} is-on{% endunless %}"
        href="{{ collection.url }}"
        {%- unless fr_filtered %} aria-current="true"{% endunless -%}
      >
        All
        <span class="onrepeat-rail__n">{{ fr_total }}</span>
      </a>

      {%- for fr_name in fr_list -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign fr_count = 0
          unless fr_filtered
            for fr_product in collection.products
              if fr_product.tags contains fr_name
                assign fr_count = fr_count | plus: 1
              endif
            endfor
          endunless

          assign fr_active = false
          if current_tags contains fr_name
            assign fr_active = true
          endif
        -%}
        <a
          class="onrepeat-rail__chip{% if fr_active %} is-on{% endif %}"
          href="{{ collection.url }}/{{ fr_name | handleize }}"
          {%- if fr_active %} aria-current="true"{% endif -%}
        >
          {{- fr_name | capitalize -}}
          {%- if fr_count > 0 -%}
            <span class="onrepeat-rail__n">{{ fr_count }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </a>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </nav>

{%- elsif fr_cats > 1 -%}
  {{ 'onrepeat-collection.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <nav class="onrepeat-rail" aria-label="Browse categories">
    {%- comment -%}
      No "All" chip here. In the family rail "All" means "clear the tag and
      show me the whole collection I am already in", which is a real place to
      go back to. Across categories there is no such parent — the chip would
      either point at whichever collection happens to be open, which is where
      you already are, or at a hardcoded catalogue URL that is one more thing
      to keep true. The current category is marked instead.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="onrepeat-rail__track">{{ fr_cat_chips }}</div>
  </nav>
{%- endif -%}
